From 5e5ca41661d9f5353291123227ae1bf0dea9139f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DSH <dsh@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 13:16:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 01/21] feat(tts): queue native TTS sentences ahead of playback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Direct-speak submits one sentence and waits for its `end` event before
submitting the next, so every sentence pays the engine's start latency as
silence — plus, on iOS, a 100 ms pre-utterance delay on every sentence.
Both engines already queue: Android has QUEUE_ADD (only reachable through
the preload path, which playback never used) and AVSpeechSynthesizer
appends natively.

Keep a small window of sentences submitted ahead, route events by
utterance id, and yield them in reading order; the first sentence of a run
still flushes so a seek cannot leave stale audio behind. A mark is
dispatched when its sentence becomes audible, not when it is queued, and
the per-sentence setVoice call is skipped while the voice is unchanged.

The window deepens while the page is hidden: the webview's timers are
throttled there, so a shallow queue would drain and stop at the next
sentence boundary.

Sentence-level latency (start delay, audible gap, dispatch overhead) is
recorded for the M0 measurements, behind `tts-latency-metrics` in
localStorage and readable through `globalThis.ttsLatencyReport()`.
