From 2724d2a40171e1be48748d2209f94562bca38d6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DSH <dsh@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 15:30:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/21] feat(tts): merge consecutive sentences into one engine
 utterance
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Queueing removed everything the client did between sentences, but not the
pause the engine itself leaves around each call: it closes and reopens its
audio track per utterance. That floor is why native read-aloud still sounds
looser than a buffered engine, whose audio is spliced sample to sample.

Read two or three sentences in one call instead. The boundary disappears
rather than being hidden, and the engine gets a longer run of text, so its
prosody carries across the sentence too. Sentence-level highlight is kept by
mapping the engine's word ranges back to the sentences merged into the
utterance.

Merging is only safe where ranges are reported, so it switches on after the
first range event and off again if a merged utterance finishes without one.
Grouping is computed once per block and cached: the preload and the later
speak must agree on it, or the queue would look discontinuous and restart.
Step and loop modes keep one sentence per call — they stop on sentence
boundaries.

Also drops the notification readout from the measurement build: it never
showed on Android, and merging is the change the numbers were for.
