From c66c83ff7f7685c542d63a0856281433fc08a3c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DSH <dsh@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 17:10:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 06/21] feat(tts): serve Azure Speech through the custom TTS
 service
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The multilingual neural voices — XiaoxiaoMultilingual, XiaochenMultilingual,
YunfanMultilingual — are not on the Edge Read Aloud endpoint: asking it for
them returns no audio at all, which a probe against the live service confirms.
They come from Azure Speech instead, and that is where the voices a user's
own TTS setup offers actually live.

Recognise an Azure host in the custom service's base URL and speak its
protocol: SSML with the voice, Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key instead of a bearer
token, and the same 24 kHz / 48 kbps MP3 the Edge path already produces. The
voice list comes from Azure's own endpoint, so the picker shows the locales
it actually serves.

The payoff is that these voices then run through BufferedTTSClient like every
other buffered engine: prefetch, gapless playout, cache, rate. No new settings
surface — the service is added the same way as any other, with the region's
endpoint as the base URL.

Errors map by cause: 401/403 is a key or region mistake and ends the session,
400 is this sentence, 429 is worth retrying.
