From e22fbdb5dc3061cb424717206e27ec860ea47426 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DSH <dsh@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 22:52:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/21] feat(tts): speak to any service through an editable
 request template
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every vendor speaks a different dialect: the endpoint, the auth header, the
body shape, the response envelope, and even the audio encoding. MiniMax is the
sharp example — it answers HTTP 200 with the failure inside the body and hands
back hex. An adapter per vendor would always lag behind, so the request itself
becomes data.

A template holds the method, URL, headers, body and how to read the answer:
a JSON path for the audio, whether it is raw/hex/base64, an optional
success field (because 200 does not always mean success) and where the
vendor's error message lives. Values come from {{text}}, {{voice}}, {{lang}},
{{rate}}, {{pitch}}, {{apiKey}} and {{baseUrl}}; they are quoted when they
land inside JSON and URL-encoded inside a URL.

The editor gains a preset picker and a protocol selector, with the template
fields folded into an advanced section. Presets ship for OpenAI, Azure,
MultiTTS, MiniMax, ElevenLabs, Fish Audio, 火山引擎, Gemini and GPT-SoVITS —
all plain data, so a wrong guess is fixable in the editor and a vendor we never
heard of needs no code at all.
